Q3. Why is Node.js single-threaded?

Node.js executes JavaScript on a main thread using an event loop.

For I/O operations, such as database queries and file operations, it can perform other tasks while waiting for the result.

Some operations use the operating system or libuv thread pool.

This makes Node.js efficient for I/O-intensive applications.

Q4. What is the Event Loop?

The event loop allows Node.js to handle asynchronous operations without blocking the main JavaScript thread.

For example, when we make a database call, Node.js can continue handling other tasks while waiting for the database response.

When the result is ready, the corresponding callback or promise continuation is processed.

Q5. What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous programming?

Synchronous: Operations execute one after another. A slow operation can block further execution.

Asynchronous: An operation can start, and the application can continue with other work while waiting for its result.

Example:

// Synchronous
const fs = require("fs");

const data = fs.readFileSync("file.txt", "utf8");

// Asynchronous
fs.readFile("file.txt", "utf8", (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return;
  }

  console.log(data);
});

Q6. What is the difference between require and import?

require is commonly used in CommonJS modules.

import is used in ES modules.

