  try {
    const users = await User.find();
    return users;
  } catch (error) {
    throw error;
  }
}

Q8. How do you handle errors in Node.js?

I use try-catch for asynchronous operations with async/await.

In Express, I use centralized error-handling middleware to handle errors consistently and return appropriate HTTP status codes.

I also log errors for debugging and monitoring.

Example:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json({
    success: false,
    message: err.statusCode
      ? err.message
      : "Internal server error"
  });
});

In Express 4, asynchronous errors need to be passed to next() or handled by an async wrapper. Express 5 automatically forwards rejected promises from route handlers to error-handling middleware.


